essageƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;9j1qmCGWUvy_-vtJ_fCjaJK_s_naINjBN_sROkyJp3Yƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarke12034ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;DskN7NC2og7eRIPo5-yRBFswHT0IN8ohOFou1v7C1RMƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;7oTJMTYeB0GtyCLu1FApS_kwV0AJpn-H3LmTb4v-BTMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;29JpR_WT8m4-KIws090ETO1pyZ7HbrwhSFEWHTRwmygƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;A8nJnP8c2-cAbt7SHGJx7TJWwzkG8zBucjlXsYsIQYoƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhstandardƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;9_1EniEXtA5vSiQcJuQ34dFc6H_6T0iKlys9Q0ggRnIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhstandardƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;DX4rsFS-zBzX4PB6IqNnt72yYZnqj-oWX5p1HZDxpOoƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$fc197473-a5f6-4ff3-abb9-3124f2f8367fƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;5vwuVwEe6FH5o7W7HXwgICtFaEfM0OQgQ4dCzN3lYB8ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky"±{"tool":"submit_revision_request","failureLayer":"writer-model","replayFrom":"writer-model","summary":"Three material factual errors introduced by the writer model require bounded repair before publication: an unsupported claim that the statue's dove was stolen multiple times (source documents one theft, in 2020); a timing misattribution placing brick vandalism \"as recently as the time of Hyperallergic's report\" when the source locates that damage during Rubin's 2023 visit; and an invented compound documentary title \"Stolen Dove: Who Killed Alex Odeh?\" that conflates the art project \"The Stolen Dove\" with the separate documentary \"Who Killed Alex Odeh?\" All three defects are bounded and repairable in place. The underlying source (Hyperallergic, 2026-07-17) is well-evidenced and the manuscript's structure, attribution, and citation practices are otherwise sound.","requiredRepairs":["Correct unsupported 'stolen more than once' claim: source documents only one dove theft (2020); manuscript must not assert multiple thefts.","Correct temporal misattribution of brick vandalism: source places brick debris during Rubin's spring 2023 visit, not at the time of the July 2026 Hyperallergic article.","Correct invented compound title 'Stolen Dove: Who Killed Alex Odeh?': source distinguishes the art project 'The Stolen Dove' from the documentary 'Who Killed Alex Odeh?' as two separate things that appeared together at Sundance."],"repairTools":[{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":"the dove was stolen more than once over subsequent years, most recently recovered and reattached in 2020","after":"the dove was stolen in 2020 and soon recovered and reattached"},{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":"As recently as the time of Hyperallergic's report, brick debris lay strewn near the sculpture following another act of vandalism.","after":""},{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":"Artist Jon Rubin came across the statue in the spring of 2023 while completing a residency at the Grand Central Art Center in Santa Ana, and was moved enough to stay on, according to Hyperallergic.","after":"Artist Jon Rubin came across the statue in the spring of 2023 while completing a residency at the Grand Central Art Center in Santa Ana â€” remnants of a brick lay strewn near the sculpture from a recent vandalism â€” and was moved enough to stay on, according to Hyperallergic."},{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":"a documentary tracing the circumstances of his assassination, *Stolen Dove: Who Killed Alex Odeh?*, had its debut at the Sundance Film Festival in January, the same source reports.","after":"the art project made its Sundance debut in January at the screening of the documentary *Who Killed Alex Odeh?*, the same source reports."},{"tool":"repair_editorial_surfaces"}],"report":"## Editorial Assessment â€” reporting-source-1-for-living-memorial-palestinian-activist-alex\n\n### Source and Evidence Basis\n\nThe single source is a Hyperallergic feature (published 2026-07-17) about artist Jon Rubin's participatory project \"The Stolen Dove,\" which removes the bronze dove from Khalil Bendib's 1994 memorial statue of Alex Odeh outside the Santa Ana Public Library and circulates it through hosted events. The source is a named-journalist article with multiple attributed quotations and specific dates. It is not confirmed as a primary document (the editorial risk flag `primary-source-unproven` applies), but it is a credible secondary source with sufficient detail to support a 545-word brief. No independently corroborating source is available, and the manuscript correctly attributes claims to Hyperallergic throughout. The source basis is adequate for the commission.\n\n### Headline and Lede Fidelity\n\nThe headline \"Dove Takes Flight, Carrying Odeh's Legacy\" is faithful to the reported event â€” the dove element of the statue is literally traveling from host to host. It intensifies relevance without overstating certainty and preserves the proper frame. The lede (first two paragraphs) names the statue, its location, its subject, and the participatory project that is the occasion for the article. Attribution is visible in the first paragraph. No complaints.\n\n### Structural and Attribution Standards\n\nThe manuscript carries no level-two section headings. The deterministic standards flagged this as a warning (`demand-lens-missing-h2`), and the commission instructs three to five `##` sections. However, the standards reading returned `publishable: true`, and the evidence budget targets 300â€“650 words â€” at 545 words, the absence of section breaks does not constitute a blocking defect under the operative reading. The warning is noted but does not require repair.\n\nAttribution is consistent throughout: every factual paragraph either names Hyperallergic inline or links to it. The citation appears as a grammatical prose sentence in the first paragraph, satisfying the citation-as-precondition rule. The source title is not quoted in link text in a way that could mutate. The constructions \"according to Hyperallergic\" and \"the same source reports\" vary acceptably throughout without becoming formulaic.\n\n### Material Claim Audit Against Witnessed Evidence\n\n**Defect 1 â€” Unsupported multiple-theft claim.** The manuscript states \"the dove was stolen more than once over subsequent years.\" The source states: \"in 2020, the dove was stolen, though it was soon recovered and reattached.\" No other dove theft is documented in the source. The source's three vandalism incidents are: red paint (1996), red paint (1997), and dove theft (2020). One dove theft is evidenced; \"more than once\" is a fabrication. Repair required.\n\n**Defect 2 â€” Brick vandalism timing misattributed.** The manuscript states \"As recently as the time of Hyperallergic's report, brick debris lay strewn near the sculpture following another act of vandalism.\" The source locates the brick debris during Rubin's spring 2023 residency visit: \"It had recently been vandalized, and remnants of a brick lay strewn on and about the sculpture.\" Framing this as contemporaneous with the July 2026 article misrepresents the source's temporal structure by approximately three years. The sentence must be removed from the vandalism chronology paragraph and the brick detail restored to its proper context â€” Rubin's 2023 encounter â€” which is the following paragraph. Repair required.\n\n**Defect 3 â€” Invented compound documentary title.** The manuscript introduces the title \"*Stolen Dove: Who Killed Alex Odeh?*\" as a documentary. The source distinguishes two distinct entities: the art project \"The Stolen Dove\" and the documentary film \"Who Killed Alex Odeh?\" â€” the project made its Sundance debut at a screening of the documentary, but they are not one combined work. The compound title the manuscript uses does not appear in the source and constitutes an invented attribution. Repair required.\n\n**Minor completeness gap (not repair-blocking).** The source states the statue depicts Odeh \"holding a book in one hand and a dove in the other.\" The manuscript's lede describes only \"a dove resting in his outstretched hand,\" omitting the book. This is a minor omission that does not constitute a falsehood and does not independently block publication given the other approved repairs.\n\n### Fairness and Harm Review\n\nThe manuscript covers Odeh's assassination and the unsolved murder case. It attributes the FBI and investigative journalists' identification of the Jewish Defense League to the source, correctly preserving the hedged \"potentially responsible\" language and stating no one has ever been arrested. The source's characterization of the JDL as a \"right-wing terrorist group\" is not reproduced in the manuscript â€” an editorial choice, but not an unfair one, since the key evidentiary claims (identification, no arrest, officially unsolved) are preserved. No party is accused beyond what the source licenses. There is no sensationalism or unattributed opinion. Advocacy quotations present in the source (connecting Odeh's assassination to \"occupation and genocide in Palestine\") are not carried into the manuscript, which keeps the brief within the observed-event frame.\n\n### Pipeline Diagnosis\n\nThe source basis was adequate. The assignment was clear and the evidence cards were sufficient. The defects (unsupported quantitative claim, temporal misattribution, invented title) are writer-model errors â€” the model exceeded or misread the available evidence rather than being compelled to by thin sources. All three defects are local and repairable with bounded text replacements. The `repair_editorial_surfaces` tool is included because the body repairs shift the brick-vandalism context and the Sundance sentence, which may affect regenerated summary and description surfaces."}ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;q5RdtrIIB-XbuQdpsMzg5tGSIowRmyW5BjokR6nGw_Qƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;4IxW3pv7t1Hns8E9djWvCoiE0HV59YgEo75PVmoLm5Qƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ddmI4xAqzDBgcsTGN8aPdFW6_XU_w69lc6lbvuK_4k4ƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;i6xLToi-uxUH8JKQ0mvAaI4koP8cbSvN1vFNjtm5zPYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxclaude-code-cli-invocationsƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;VofIDP7T6U3MhapPGD_D-PrWxPjToiSZe4lDaE04HU0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka1ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;T5FE_jrQi9ZP6x9AE6rkxUBICiGDsZU2tBrJuTdFFJsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkg189.768ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;siIODjrf_cEGt275TWPo5mTIHqzcgJyyjJFjBMAGPHcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;wML-aAMYyFDSKSy099H3Q5NIL9pOr2sjQlCz6Z-zDtcƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;PtDBDUuAFrfR_yvtr0qHuLXcRg29O2